+### Correction added 19 September 2026: separate domain separation from tree shape
+
+The earlier explanation incorrectly attributed the duplicate-last padding fix to the leaf/node prefixes alone. Prefixing leaves with `0x00` and interior nodes with `0x01` does not remove the demonstrated ambiguity while duplicate-last padding is retained.
+
+RFC 6962 section 2.1 defines both separate hash domains and a recursive tree shape: split at the largest power of two smaller than the list length, without duplicating an unmatched tail. These are distinct properties. The recursive shape distinguishes a list from the list with its last entry duplicated; domain separation provides the separate leaf/interior second-preimage property.
+
+For the existing duplicate-last construction, a separately authenticated count distinguishes the specific presentations in this note. Count binding alone is not a general collection-membership proof, and one inclusion proof is not append-only consistency evidence. A verifier must apply the exact declared construction, authenticated size and applicable proof rules.
+
+The promotion comparison below remains a historical report of that particular experiment, not a recommendation that promotion alone is a complete security fix. Any future migration must declare the full hashing and tree rules and preserve the original signed records under their historical rules. This explanatory correction does not change roots, signatures, witnesses, proofs or historical test outputs and does not claim a deployed v2 construction.
